 and diligence concerns, **not an allegation of fraud**. The main FY26 financial-statement and IFC opinions are unmodified. Both statements must be kept in view. [S01, PDF120–133,152; S06, PDF1–5]\\n\\nAt the secondary ₹420 reference, current-share market capitalization is ₹447.06 crore and historical P/E approximately 39.83x. This is not a bargain established by the IPO-era EPS denominator. All projected outcomes below are conditional, and no ordinary return is reported for a cash-breaching path. There is no live investability score or probability-weighted target.\\n\\n## 2. Research header, gates and data confidence\\n\\n| Item | Treatment |\\n|---|---|\\n| Exact issuer | Recode Studios Limited, CIN L74999PB2021PLC053619; ISIN INE2B6701015; BSE SME RECODE, scrip 544755. Not a software company or another “Recode.” |\\n| History | Incorporated 16 June 2021; converted to public company 16 September 2025; listing date 12 May 2026. [S01/S02] |\\n| Capital | 10,644,344 current issued shares; 800-share lot confirmed by official 19 October interoperability security master. The master is not an NSE listing or an exchange quote. [S08] |\\n| Price | ₹420 on 19 October from IIFL historical HTML, secondary and conditional. Official BSE bhavcopy unavailable with ordinary HTTP403. [S09/X01] |\\n| Scope | FY22 stub and FY23–FY26 standalone accounts; FY23–FY25 restated prospectus figures are separate from annual-report comparators. A post-year 33% associate is not consolidated at 100%. |\\n| Horizon / hurdle | Five years, 19 October 2026–19 October 2031; default 15% nominal annualized before investor tax. WACC is separately assumed, not equated with that hurdle. |\\n| Liquidity reference | ₹5 lakh, purely illustrative, not a suggested position. |\\n| Overall confidence | Low for investment conclusion. Medium for historical revenue trajectory; low for current cash, normalized earnings, incremental returns and liquidity. |\\n\\n| Gate | Status | What closes the gap |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| G1 Integrity / minority treatment | Conditional | Explain audit/control contradictions, related-party/IP assignments and remuneration; no substantiated ≤3 integrity override applied. |\\n| G2 Financial reliability | Hold | Corrected final CARO and reconciled bank, tax and cash-flow notes, with auditor-supported bridges. |\\n| G3 Solvency / funding | Hold | Current unrestricted and object-restricted bank cash; monitoring report; acquisition payment/phase-II commitments; facility maturity and covenant schedules. |\\n| G4 Tradability | Hold | Authoritative complete 20/60-session trading history, actual volume, zero-trade days, circuits/spread and current restrictions. |\\n\\nA modeled cash failure is not evidence that the company actually defaulted. Conversely, an assumed funded scenario cannot establish current solvency. The distinction is maintained throughout.\\n\\n## 3. Scorecard and calculation audit\\n\\n| Factor | Score | Confidence | Evidence and rationale |\\n|---|---:|---|---|\\n| GOV | 4 | Medium | S01/S02/S06: Paid Section42 private-placement compliance penalties, FY24 edit-log exception, FY26 unfinished CARO drafting and bank mismatches constrain confidence. No proven fraud/minority abuse inferred. |\\n| MOAT | 4 | Medium | S02/S07: Brand and channel access, no independently proven retention/CAC advantage or proprietary manufacturing; trademark assignments not fully reflected in registry. |\\n| REV | 8 | Medium | S02/S01: Three-year FY23-FY26 comparable operating-revenue CAGR exceeds30%; anchor9 capped8 for fewer than5 relevant full years and Medium confidence. No extra trajectory point. |\\n| DUR | 6 | Medium | S02/S07: Beauty consumption and omnichannel reach support runway; marketplace competition, supplier concentration and unproved repeat economics constrain durability. |\\n| CAP | NS | Low | S01/S02/S07: Mechanical returns can be computed but cohort and incremental capital economics, current deployment and accounting bridges insufficient. |\\n| CASH | 3 | Medium | S01/S02: FY23-FY25 cumulative CFO negative; FY23-FY26 reported CFO only a small fraction of PAT. Latest cash bridge is unreconciled. Persistent weakness triggers GQS cap5. |\\n| BAL | NS | Low | S01/S02/S10: Audited March cash not current October cash; IPO deployment, acquisition payment terms and demand facilities not reconciled. |\\n| EXEC | 5 | Medium | S01/S02/S07: Sales expansion achieved; franchise versus dark-store pivot, warehouse future delivery and M&A require testing. |\\n| EARN | NS | Low | S01/S02: Restatements, cash-flow presentation and tax-note mismatch prevent high-confidence normalized earnings assessment. |\\n| R | NS | Low | S09/model: Conditional model returns only; price and cash path not sufficiently verified for live scoring. |\\n| D | NS | Low | S01/model: Permanent-loss paths identifiable, but current funding gap and acquisition commitments ND. |\\n| P | NS | Low | S01/S07/model: Price-implied expectations cannot be underwritten reliably: secondary price, unverified current funding and demanding reverse-growth/margin paths. Sensitivity robustness insufficient for live scoring. |\\n| L | NS | Low | S08/S09: Only18 secondary observed trading rows. Authoritative20/60-session liquidity and restrictions unavailable. |\\n\\nThe nine business factors and four investment factors above are the complete 13-factor register. The three composites remain distinct. Every multiplication uses Decimal inputs and ROUND_HALF_UP only at final display, with no renormalization of missing factors.\\n\\n- **BQS:** 0.20×4 + 0.20×4 + 0.12×8 + 0.10×6 + 0.12×NS + 0.10×3 + 0.08×NS + 0.08×5 = known contribution **3.86**, coverage **80%**, missing weight **20%**. Mathematical bounds **4.06–5.86**; result **NS**, not 4.8/10 or a rescaled average.\\n- **GQS:** 0.40×8 + 0.20×6 + 0.15×NS + 0.15×NS + 0.10×3 = known contribution **4.70**, coverage **70%**, missing weight **30%**. Uncapped bounds **5.00–7.70**. Persistent weak cash conversion triggers a **5** cap, collapsing admissible numeric bounds to **5.00–5.00**, but the missing inputs still make the result **NS**, not a 5 score.\\n- **IAS:** 0.25×NS BQS + 0.35×NS R + 0.20×NS D + 0.10×NS P + 0.10×NS L. Direct coverage **0%**, direct formal bounds **1–10**; nested BQS information narrows possible arithmetic to **1.765–8.965**, not a score. No live ranking.\\n\\nREV's preselected relevant window is FY23–FY26, four full-year observations and three years of growth. Revenue CAGR 52.87% and core-product CAGR 57.83% exceed the >30% anchor, but the score is capped at 8 for fewer than five comparable full years/Medium confidence. The mixed FY22 stub is not used to manufacture a five-year CAGR. No additional momentum point is added. No governance≤3 or proven G3-failure override is asserted; such caps are policy rules, not findings supported by these data. Exact terms, bounds and cap order are in `score_audit.json`. A GOV±1 judgment sensitivity leaves all missing factors NS: GOV3 invokes BQS/IAS cap3; GOV5 changes BQS bounds to4.26–6.06 without making it scoreable. A separate hypothetical proven G3 failure caps IAS3, again without imputing missing inputs. These are boundary tests, not new factual classifications.\\n\\n## 4. Business overview\\n\\nRecode is an **own-brand cosmetics sourcing, trading and distribution business**. It is neither an outsourced manufacturer for other brands nor a proprietary technology platform. It sells face, eye, lip and body products and related items, with approximately 350 SKUs. Production is sourced from 11 domestic and six overseas manufacturers, including suppliers in Germany, Taiwan, China and Thailand; there is no demonstrated owned factory or manufacturing-process moat. Product quality, formulation consistency, inventory availability and supplier economics matter more than nominal asset-lightness. [S02, business section, PDF139–153]\\n\\nChannels are owned website/marketplaces, B2B distributors and retailers, franchise stores (FOFO) and company-operated stores (COCO). The consistent reported footprint is three COCO and 19 FOFO; another prospectus passage says 21 FOFO, so the count discrepancy is retained rather than harmonized without evidence. FOFO stores may also distribute to smaller shops. Consequently wholesale sell-in, franchise consumer retail sales and marketplace GMV are not interchangeable revenue measures. [S02, business/risk sections; S07, 3 June call]\\n\\n| Net-product revenue | FY23 | FY24 | FY25 | 9M Dec25 |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| COCO | 0.00 | 0.31 | 0.87 | 0.51 |\\n| FOFO sell-in | 0.80 | 3.32 | 10.96 | 13.27 |\\n| Online | 8.68 | 12.23 | 20.02 | 24.84 |\\n| B2B | 10.18 | 17.78 | 14.86 | 16.89 |\\n| Net products | 19.66 | 33.64 | 46.70 | 55.52 |\\n\\nThese are disclosed historical amounts, not channel contribution margins. FY24 and 9M component totals differ from stated product totals by ₹0.01 lakh rounding; some printed FY24 channel percentages do not match the amounts. Calculations use amounts and disclose the discrepancy. Packing/service/marketing-support revenues are separate: FY26 net products ₹77.31 crore, franchise/services ₹0.18 crore and packing ₹2.47 crore. FY23 revenue included ₹1.34 crore of marketing support, making product-only growth a useful cross-check. [S02, channel tables; S01, revenue note]\\n\\n## 5. Moat and industry position\\n\\nThe plausible advantage is a recognizable affordable cosmetics brand with professional/influencer reach and a distributor/franchise network. It remains a **hypothesis of moderate brand strength, not an established economic moat**. Consumers can switch; external manufacturing is replicable; marketplace demand can be rented through promotions; and franchise rollout is not proof of attractive same-store returns.\\n\\nThe IP record is not fully cleanly vested in the listed company. Prospectus trademarks include registrations held by Lush Looks and Bhuvan Mahajan, with executed transfer instruments claimed but registry changes pending. Some applications, including PREP+PRIME, were opposed/objected. Registry perfection, assignment terms, consideration, exclusivity and future royalty rights require verification. No valuable exclusive formulation patent or owned production technology is established. This is an IP/governance diligence issue, not an assertion that sales are unlawful. [S02, trademark/risk and statutory information, PDF145–149 and272–275]\\n\\nSupplier concentration increased: top-five share roughly 47.35% FY23, 57.02% FY24, 64.41% FY25 and 69.93% in 9M Dec25; top-ten suppliers reached 87.43% in that stub. Top-ten customers represented about 39.65% of FY25 and 39.38% of 9M revenue. Sourcing leverage, currency exposure, formulation continuity and a disruption at a major supplier can outweigh nominal vendor count. No durable minimum-volume customer commitments were verified. [S02, concentration risk tables]\\n\\nThree relevant comparators are useful economically, not as a license to transplant multiples:\\n\\n1. **Honasa Consumer:** a listed digital-first/omnichannel house of beauty brands. Relevant for cohort repeat, channel inventory and brand reinvestment; broader brand portfolio and reporting history make direct SME valuation comparisons weak. [S12]\\n2. **FSN E-Commerce Ventures / Nykaa:** a beauty retail/platform/distribution operator with owned brands. Relevant both as a channel and competitor, but gross retail revenue, marketplace economics and own-brand economics must not be conflated. [S13]\\n3. **Hindustan Unilever's Beauty & Wellbeing/Lakmé ecosystem:** a superior scale/distribution benchmark, not a small-cap peer. FY26 segment disclosure reports 6% underlying sales growth, ₹14,990 crore revenue and 28% segment margin. Segment margin is not comparable to Recode's standalone EBITDA or PAT. Mature scale and route-to-market advantages explain why its economics cannot be assumed for a young brand. [S11]\\n\\nNo synchronized peer valuation dataset was verified. Peer P/E medians, market-share gains and a precise addressable-market share are therefore ND rather than invented.\\n\\n## 6. Revenue growth and scale\\n\\n| Period | Revenue | PAT | CFO | Gross cash capex | Ending debt |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| FY22_stub | 11.11 | 0.44 | ND | ND | 1.38 |\\n| FY23 | 22.38 | 0.69 | -5.27 | 0.81 | 3.79 |\\n| FY24 | 36.82 | 0.27 | -1.64 | 1.84 | 7.85 |\\n| FY25 | 47.80 | 3.30 | 3.14 | 1.06 | 7.56 |\\n| 9M_Dec25 | 57.39 | 9.06 | 5.97 | 1.70 | 3.43 |\\n| FY26 | 79.95 | 11.22 | 4.34 | 1.83 | 6.19 |\\n\\nFY22 is the 9 September 2021–31 March 2022 operating stub, with cosmetics, electronics, healthcare/other trading mixed together. It is not a full-year pure-beauty baseline. FY23–FY25 are restated prospectus data. FY26 is current annual-report data; the 9M row is not annualized or added to FY26. Detailed original-versus-restated figures are preserved in `historical_inputs.json`. [S04; S02; S01]\\n\\nFY26 operating revenue rose 67.27% from FY25. Core product sales rose faster than total reported revenue across FY23–FY26, reducing the concern that growth is only a service/reclassification artifact. The expansion predates the post-FY26 Aflairza acquisition; the historical FY26 increase is therefore not consolidation-led. However, “organic” does not mean volume growth: audited unit volumes, price/mix, discounts, returns, same-store sales and new-store contributions are not separately reconciled. The defensible statement is **reported standalone, predominantly core-product expansion**, not a precise organic volume CAGR. [S01/S02]\\n\\nThe mix also changed sharply: FY26 gross face-product sales ₹52.97 crore versus ₹24.43 crore in FY25, while gross body-product sales fell from ₹10.69 crore to ₹6.88 crore. Net product sales deduct discounts, so gross category tables must not be mixed with net channel totals. Face strength could reflect hero products, distribution or promotions; sustainable share gain is not independently proven. [S01, Note20]\\n\\nManagement's June call indicated a roughly 50% FY27 revenue-growth ambition, later extending that ambition across three years in Q&A. It is an M-type statement, not an audited forecast or model entitlement. NewU modern-trade entry, Nykaa Now/Superstore and partner dark stores expand access but offer no verified cohort profitability yet. Historical growth gets credit; unearned future margins do not. [S07; S01 front business review; S10 announcement index]\\n\\n## 7. Growth quality and reinvestment runway\\n\\nAsset-light manufacturing does not mean capital-light growth. Recode must finance receivables, inventory, marketing, supplier commitments and physical distribution; capital can migrate from factory PPE into working capital and acquisitions. FY26 receivables rose from ₹8.09 crore to ₹15.38 crore, about 90%, above revenue growth. Ending receivable days rose from roughly 61.8 to70.2, while average-balance days are a different metric and must not be used to conceal year-end expansion. Inventory averaged roughly118 days of FY26 COGS. [S01/S02; reproducible calculations]\\n\\nThe mechanical NOPAT/ending-capital and incremental ΔNOPAT/Δcapital ratios are in `financial_calculations.json`. Operating invested capital is independently reconstructed from AR, inventory, operating advances, operating accruals/employee provisions and PPE/capital advances, excluding taxes, IPOprepayments and financing interest. Financing-minus-operating residuals, average ROIC/ROCE and incremental returns are retained, not forced to zero. Both before-interest FCF and CFO less actual financing-classified interest and capex are shown. They are not accepted as reliable cohort returns: profits jumped from a small FY24 base, tax/restatement vintages differ, IPO capital arrived after the anchor, supplier/customer financing changed, and current cash deployment is unknown. High apparent incremental returns driven by margin recovery do not prove that the next ₹1 reinvested will earn the same return. CAP remains NS.\\n\\n| Year | Operating IC | Financing IC residual | Average ROIC | Incremental ROIC | After-interest/capex cash |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| FY23 | 8.31 | 0.23 | NS | NS | -6.43 |\\n| FY24 | 12.91 | 0.25 | 8.03% | -1.41% | -4.34 |\\n| FY25 | 15.76 | -0.25 | 28.72% | 114.38% | 0.88 |\\n| FY26 | 24.52 | 0.35 | 58.09% | 86.59% | 1.84 |\\n\\nAll capital returns use an analytical25% tax rate. FY23 lacks a comparable opening pure-brand operating-capital base, so average/incremental entries are NS rather than fabricated. Residuals reflect tax balances, IPOprepay, financing interest and accounting classifications; they are not operating profits.\\n\\nThe June call supplies useful but incomplete operating leads:\\n- A claimed 40% monthly repeat rate applies to the website, not all channels or a multi-year customer cohort.\\n- Average order value was described as roughly ₹800 versus ₹700. Product mix, discounting and retention contribution are not independently bridged.\\n- Management gave inconsistent website shares of online sales, above70% in one passage and roughly50% in another. Do not select the higher figure.\\n- Customer acquisition cost was not properly attributable because influencer links lacked consistent tracking. A marketing-spend/revenue proxy is not CAC or lifetime-value evidence.\\n- Franchise monthly retail sales of ₹4–5 lakh and a22-month payback were management claims, not store-level audited cash cohorts. Franchise fees, working inventory, closures and partner returns require full denominator data.\\n- The company pivoted away from new FOFO expansion toward partner/company dark stores after the prospectus emphasized franchise rollout. The changed capital/opex burden needs a revised plan.\\n- Quick commerce was described as currently loss-making. A sale-or-return arrangement discussed for Nykaa transfers economic risk differently from a final retail sale. Promotional giveaways may dispose of slow stock but do not demonstrate zero economic obsolescence. [S07, complete call]\\n\\nIPO proceeds are treated as object-earmarked cash in the model, not asserted to be legally frozen bank balances; actual object utilization and restrictions remain unverified. IPO net proceeds were ₹33.6073 crore: warehouse ₹5.7420, brand/marketing ₹5.4090, working capital ₹19.5000 and GCP ₹2.9563. Marketing is an operating cost, not an additional capex deduction after it has already reduced modeled margin. The warehouse's total project cost ₹6.2810 crore includes ₹0.2500 crore already advanced at March2026; remaining cash outlay is ₹6.0310 crore, with remaining internal funding₹0.2890 crore. The model avoids charging the prior advance twice. [S02, objects PDF94 onward; S01 capex notes]\\n\\nAflairza's PhaseI33% investment at ₹8.32 crore is mentioned in the annual report; PhaseII51% is conditional on milestones. Target predecessor revenue and future consolidation claims are not accepted without acquired financial statements and transaction terms. A33% stake does not authorize100% revenue consolidation. The model gives no acquired operating earnings, stress-tests additional cash only and permits a separately haircut terminal recovery value. [S01 business review; X05 inaccessible transaction filing]\\n\\n## 8. Management, governance and execution\\n\\nPromoters named on the offer document are Dheeraj Bansal, Rahul Sachdeva, Shelly Bansal, Shalini Trehan, Preeti Trehan and Karan Bansal; responsibilities, remuneration and related parties are described in the prospectus. Post-IPO promoter ownership was about65.01% in the offer document. A later September ownership figure and current pledges/encumbrances were not independently verified, so no zero-pledge assertion is made. [S02, capital and management sections]\\n\\nThe15 December2025 bonus of780 for1 changed10,424 shares into8,141,144 and is not economic dilution. The IPO fresh issue added2,503,200 shares;319,200 OFS shares transferred existing ownership and provided **no company cash**. At₹158, fresh gross proceeds are₹39.55056 crore and OFS₹5.04336 crore. A2023 allotment of424 shares at₹95,700 before the bonus equates to about₹122.54 per post-bonus share, not₹95,700 in the current denominator. [S02 capital history]\\n\\nFY26 director compensation was about₹1.6925 crore, approximately15.1% of reported PAT, before other related-party family salary/rent items. Related-party rental payments to four named family counterparties total about₹0.1638 crore. The FY25 employee-cost director figure₹1.0050 crore differs from the RPT total₹1.1610 crore; the FY26₹0.0001 crore difference looks like rounding but the prior-year difference is material. These require reconciliation and arm's-length assessment, not an automatic abuse allegation. [S01, Notes23/31]\\n\\nA300,000-option ESOP scheme is reported, but current grants, vesting, exercise prices and fully diluted obligations were not verified in a primary accessible filing. The model conservatively stress-adds300,000 shares, assumes **no exercise cash**, and does not describe all scheme capacity as granted. Authorized11,000,000 shares minus issued10,644,344 and that reserve leaves55,656 shares of theoretical rescue capacity. It is not permission or a commitment to issue shares. [S01 capital note; X04 unavailable]\\n\\nExecution is mixed: strong historical sales and channel expansion are visible, but warehouse commissioning, dark-store economics, acquisitions and controls remain to be demonstrated. The prospectus discloses Section42 non-compliance in the2023 private placement: subscription money used before formal Board allotment, a third-party remittance mismatch and late PAS3. ROC orders of18 December2025 imposed company penalties totaling₹2.23 lakh and₹1.23 lakh each on Rahul Sachdeva and Dheeraj Bansal, disclosed as paid. This is a documented governance lapse, not merely speculative risk, but does not by itself establish fraud. The company was claimant in a₹4.03 lakh criminal case; two promoter tax demands totaled₹1.38 lakh. At31 December2025, disclosed bank guarantees/tax/other contingencies were nil, but a₹2.15 crore warehouse-land commitment existed; it is within the warehouse project, not an additional double-counted commitment. [S02, printed30–32,60 and262 onward]\\n\\nThe FY26 auditor change to RMSG from the earlier Samarth M. Surana firm is recorded; no adverse resignation motive is inferred without the actual transition documents.\\n\\n## 9. Financial forensics\\n\\n### Assurance history and annexures\\n\\n| Year/source | What was actually reviewed | Interpretation |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| FY22 original S04 PDF4–7 | Samarth M. Surana/Bharat Rattan,21 September2022; unmodified BS/P&L/notes opinion; CARO exemption; no standalone positive IFC report | Does not audit a cash-flow statement that was not provided. |\\n| FY23 posted S05 pack |15-page Board/AGM/tax material, no actual statutory audit or complete financial statements | Board's assertion of no qualification is not independent original-audit review. Prospectus restatement coverage is separate. |\\n| FY24 original S06 PDF1–5 | Unmodified BS/P&L/notes opinion dated2 September2024; CARO exempt; three branches lacked edit logs, auditor unable to comment there; proper-books reporting excepts that issue | Historic audit-trail weakness survives any later unmodified restatement language. No separate IFC assurance inferred. |\\n| FY25 original S03 PDF39–47 | Unmodified BS/P&L/CFO opinion; CARO read; no default/fraud/fresh allotment/loans or investments/resignation during that year reported; edit logs operated and were preserved | Statements are audit conclusions within their scope, not guarantees. Generic internal-control responsibilities are not a positive IFC opinion. |\\n| Prospectus S02 PDF186–194 and notes | RMSG restated FY23–FY25/9MDec25 report and statements, with restatement reconciliation | Restated figures deliberately differ from originals; this does not erase old control exceptions. |\\n| FY26 S01 PDF120–133 | Unmodified main and IFC opinions; CARO and legal reporting read in full | Unfinished CARO drafting, remuneration scope limitation and inconsistent clauses require correction before clean-reliability conclusion. |\\n\\n### Material contradictions and what not to do\\n\\n1. **CARO draft residue:** FY26 retains a draft-review instruction relating to agreeing highlighted conclusions with audit evidence. Separately, the auditor could not comment on adequacy of profits under Section197(16) without conversion-date statements. Do not compress this into “clean audit.” [S01, PDF122/124]\\n2. **Bank statements versus books:** March2026 reported net-current-asset book value₹21.3850 crore versus bank submission₹19.0966 crore, gap₹2.2884 crore. June2025 gap₹3.3755 crore; other quarter differences change direction and size. CARO's language on working-capital limits below₹5 crore does not reconcile the actual disclosure. [S01, Note35 PDF152]\\n3. **Tax mismatch:** tax note total₹3.6942 crore includes a prior-period credit₹0.0916 crore, while the P&L tax₹3.7858 crore and PAT₹11.2248 crore do not. Do not silently boost PAT by the credit. [S01, PDF135/148]\\n4. **Cash-flow anomalies:** advances are a₹1.6915 crore inflow even though short-term advances increase by₹1.8585 crore. Cash tax₹7.6982 crore is far above current tax charge₹3.8962 crore and not explained by tax-provision changes. Offsetting reclassifications are possible. Reported CFO is retained and confidence reduced; “corrected CFO” is ND. [S01, PDF134/136]\\n5. **Equity wording:** CARO refers to private placement despite a bonus-only FY26 share reconciliation. One note calls₹158 the premium, whereas issue terms clearly specify₹158 total and₹148 premium. Use the verified issue terms and keep the contradictory wording. [S01, PDF128/141; S02]\\n6. **Restatements:** FY23 PAT₹0.7009→₹0.6928 crore; FY24₹0.4060→₹0.2743; FY25₹3.1118→₹3.3029. Tax, MSME interest, prior items and small depreciation/FX changes explain disclosed adjustments. FY26 opening retained earnings carries₹0.0514 crore reconciliation, while comparators remain largely original. FY25 CFO₹3.1397 crore restated versus₹3.0245 crore annual comparator. [S02 restatement notes; S01]\\n7. **Supplier financing:** MSME interest payable₹0.1133 crore versus₹0.0487 crore, with expense₹0.0244 crore not alone bridging the change. Supplier credit is not free capital. [S01, Note34]\\n\\nFY26 CFO is before financing-classified interest. From₹4.3378 crore CFO, deduct₹1.8282 crore cash capex,₹0.6722 crore interest and₹1.3686 crore principal to get about₹0.4688 crore before the₹0.0265 crore net securities proceeds. This reconciles approximately₹0.4953 crore cash increase, subject to₹0.0001 crore rounding. CFO/PAT is therefore not after-interest owner cash. Gross cash capex comprises₹1.5782 crore PPE additions plus₹0.2500 crore capital advances; no acquisition is buried in that audited FY26 bridge. [S01, PDF136 and PPE notes]\\n\\nReceivables more than six months overdue were₹0.8188 crore, about5.3% of total, versus₹0.6886 crore/about8.5% previously. Rising absolute balances do not mean every aging percentage worsened. More than one year was₹0.3918 crore. Inventory obsolescence, returns and distributor stock require cohort evidence beyond a management statement about giveaways. [S01, receivable aging]\\n\\nMarch2026 debt₹6.1927 crore includes term/current maturities₹2.9369 crore and demand borrowing₹3.2558 crore; cash₹1.3170 crore gives net debt₹4.8757 crore. Cash consists of bank balances₹1.0601 crore and cash on hand₹0.2569 crore. IPO money later raised cannot simply be added to March cash and called October net cash. Facility renewals are not committed long-term finance. Operating leases are already expensed under AS; no lease-debt addback is made without adjusting rent. [S01, notes3–19 and loan maturity note]\\n\\n## 10. Valuation and market expectations\\n\\nAt the secondary reference, market cap is₹447.06 crore. FY26 PAT divided by10,644,344 current shares gives EPS₹10.55, not the report's pre-IPO₹13.79. P/E is approximately39.83x. A March balance-sheet EV combined with an October price would be a mixed-date statistic; no falsely precise current EV or “ex-IPO cash” multiple is claimed.\\n\\n### Cash-linked model, not a free-standing terminal multiple\\n\\nThe model begins at the audited31 March2026 balance sheet and explicitly bridges pre-entry dates through19 October, then60 holding months to19 October2031. Sales, collections, AR, inventory, supplier balances, other working capital, tax, interest, maintenance/growth capex, warehouse spend, dividends, acquisitions, debt maturities and restricted cash releases are linked. Each event pays outlays after an assumed early collection fraction and before late collections. A minimum cash breach ends that path; later profits cannot retroactively repair it.\\n\\nAll interim operating assumptions are **A**, not actual FY27 results. Base entry revenue run-rate₹119.92 crore rises to₹135 crore after12 holding months and22% annually thereafter; bear₹95→₹100 crore/6%; bull₹125→₹160 crore/30%. These are run-rates, not claimed annual revenue forecasts. The model decomposes each annual exit run-rate into25% own-site,25% marketplace,18% FOFO sell-in,30% B2B and2% COCO, with own-site orders at₹800 AOV. This is an explicit feasibility exercise, not a verified channel mix or consumer cohort. No retail/wholesale double count is used.\\n\\n| Parameter | Bear | Base | Bull |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\\n| EBITDA margin |12%|20%|23%|\\n| COGS/revenue |45%|40%|38%|\\n| AR days |100|65|52|\\n| Inventory days |190|130|100|\\n| Payable days |30|50|60|\\n| Early share of collections |70%|93%|98%|\\n| Terminal WACC |18%|15%|13%|\\n| Terminal growth |2%|4%|5%|\\n| Terminal ROIC ceiling |15%|25%|30%|\\n| Associate cost recovery |0%|50%|100%|\\n\\nMaintenance capex is1.2% of sales; growth capex is3.5% of the excess annual run-rate over the reference base, multiplied by elapsed-year fraction. Depreciation is1.2% of sales; tax25%; debt interest12%. These assumptions are independently visible in inputs, not calibrated to produce a target price. Warehouse remaining₹6.031 crore is scheduled byApril2027. Object funds are released only for matching expenses/working-capital additions; unused object cash is excluded from terminal shareholder cash. Net IPO proceeds exclude all issue fees, but₹0.4028 crore already prepaid before the anchor is reimbursed once to avoid double deduction. OFS provides no funding. The planned marketing object is released against operating expenses already inside margin, not deducted twice.\\n\\nThe PhaseI₹8.32 crore acquisition is assigned a23 July2026 cash event as an explicit timing assumption pending the SPA/bank evidence. Base and bull assume no mandatory PhaseII funding; bear assumes₹5 crore. Separate₹5/10 crore stresses test the unknown future commitment. A pre-entry₹0.25-per-share dividend (₹0.2661 crore) is a company cash outflow; an investor entering19 October does not receive it. No subsequent dividend is assumed.\\n\\n| Scenario | Cash status | First breach | Terminal price | Five-year IRR | Rescue result |\\n|---|---|---|---:|---:|---|\\n| bear | CASH_BREACH | 2026-04-30 | Not admissible | Not calculated | PREENTRY_BREACH_CANNOT_RESCUE_AT_ENTRY |\\n| base | FUNDED_CONDITIONAL | None in assumed path | 383.53 | -1.80% | NO_RESCUE_NEEDED |\\n| bull | FUNDED_CONDITIONAL | None in assumed path | 872.77 | 15.75% | NO_RESCUE_NEEDED |\\n\\n\\nTerminal enterprise value is earned from **forward12-month NOPAT ×(1−g/earned incremental ROIC)/(WACC−g)**. Forward NOPAT is the exact sum of monthly ramped sales less operating costs, depreciation and assumed tax, not terminal run-rate NOPAT mechanically multiplied by(1+g). ROIC is the lower of the scenario ceiling and terminal NOPAT divided by actual modeled operating invested capital. Reinvestment is not set to zero while growth remains positive. Add only free cash above the₹0.25 crore operating floor and a haircut associate value; subtract remaining debt. A further12-month monthly cash continuation, with no new financing or object release, proves terminal operating funding. It maintains working-capital/PPE intensity and adds productive-capital spending where needed to respect the conservative incremental ROIC ceiling. The explicit net investment equals Δworking capital + growth PPE + that additional investment; Gordon reinvestment uses this amount, not an unsupported profit payout. Required reserve is the cash floor plus the worst cumulative early/late-collection draw in that continuation, and is excluded from excess cash. Any continuation breach suppresses ordinary returns; subsequent continuation rows, if retained to diagnose required reserve, are explicitly marked post-breach counterfactual diagnostics and never a resumed funded path. No residual restricted object cash is counted as freely distributable.300,000 potential ESOP shares are included as a dilution stress without assuming exercise proceeds. Implied P/E and EV/EBITDA are outputs, never starting assumptions.\\n\\n### Whole-share rescue and return thresholds\\n\\nRescue is an **uncommitted counterfactual**, not ordinary financing. It can occur only at entry, after the pre-entry path survives, at30% discount to the tested share price with5% issue cost. Maximum55,656 whole shares reflects existing authorized capacity after the ESOP reserve, approximately₹1.55 crore net at₹420. Every candidate is rerun through the full cash path. Because operating cash flows are independent of the number of rescue shares, the solver uses exact cash linearity and Decimal ceiling to obtain the smallest whole-share candidate within the bound, then reruns that candidate and its adjacent failed share count. The no-rescue, maximum-capacity, minimum-success and adjacent-failure paths are all retained. Pre-entry failures cannot be repaired by issuing shares after the failure.\\n\\n| Scenario | Hurdle | Retested threshold | Funding status |\\n|---|---:|---:|---|\\n| bear | 12.00% | ND | NO_FUNDED_TERMINAL |\\n| bear | 15.00% | ND | NO_FUNDED_TERMINAL |\\n| bear | 18.00% | ND | NO_FUNDED_TERMINAL |\\n| base | 12.00% | 217.62 | CONDITIONAL_NOT_ACTIONABLE |\\n| base | 15.00% | 190.68 | CONDITIONAL_NOT_ACTIONABLE |\\n| base | 18.00% | 167.64 | CONDITIONAL_NOT_ACTIONABLE |\\n| bull | 12.00% | 495.23 | CONDITIONAL_NOT_ACTIONABLE |\\n| bull | 15.00% | 433.92 | CONDITIONAL_NOT_ACTIONABLE |\\n| bull | 18.00% | 381.49 | CONDITIONAL_NOT_ACTIONABLE |\\n\\nThese are conditional arithmetic thresholds, not buy prices. The financing model is retested at each threshold because a lower subscription price can reduce rescue cash. The hurdle is an equity-return assumption, not the WACC. No precise entry threshold becomes actionable while G2/G3/G4 remain unresolved.\\n\\n| Hurdle | Joint price/dilution bracket for PhaseII10cr stress | Status |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| 12.00% | 214.5552 to 214.5554 | JOINT_PRICE_DILUTION_BRACKET |\\n| 15.00% | 187.8975 to 187.8976 | JOINT_PRICE_DILUTION_BRACKET |\\n| 18.00% | 165.1037 to 165.1038 | JOINT_PRICE_DILUTION_BRACKET |\\n\\nFor the₹10 crore PhaseII stress, entry price and whole-share rescue dilution are additionally solved jointly. Every price trial recalculates the minimum shares and reruns the cash path; a price below the financing floor is inadmissible even if a naïve terminal-price division suggests a high return. Integer dilution can produce tiny jumps, so bounded price intervals are reported instead of spurious exact roots.\\n\\n### Reverse expectations and sensitivities\\n\\n| Hurdle | Conditional growth bracket | Status |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| 12.00% | 50.65% to 50.65% | BOUNDED_CONDITIONAL_ROOT |\\n| 15.00% | 56.68% to 56.68% | BOUNDED_CONDITIONAL_ROOT |\\n| 18.00% | 62.67% to 62.67% | BOUNDED_CONDITIONAL_ROOT |\\n\\nReverse growth searches0–80% annual post-first-year growth. Sampled feasible/infeasible boundaries are refined before testing a return-root bracket, avoiding a false no-root conclusion from a coarse grid. All grid, boundary-refinement, bisection and failed funding trials remain in the losslessly deduplicated path register. A root is rejected if it crosses a cash infeasibility discontinuity; no algebraic growth root is accepted without a corresponding funded trajectory. Hurdle roots also retain integer rescue subtrials.\\n\\n| Stress | Ordinary cash result | First breach | Five-year IRR if funded | Bounded rescue |\\n|---|---|---|---:|---|\\n| margin_minus_3pp | FUNDED_CONDITIONAL | None | -5.60% | NO_RESCUE_NEEDED |\\n| margin_plus_3pp | FUNDED_CONDITIONAL | None | 1.49% | NO_RESCUE_NEEDED |\\n| AR_plus_30days | FUNDED_CONDITIONAL | None | -2.98% | NO_RESCUE_NEEDED |\\n| inventory_plus_60days | FUNDED_CONDITIONAL | None | -2.74% | NO_RESCUE_NEEDED |\\n| supplier_days_minus_20 | FUNDED_CONDITIONAL | None | -2.11% | NO_RESCUE_NEEDED |\\n| early_collections_70pct | CASH_BREACH | 2026-04-30 | Not calculated | PREENTRY_BREACH_CANNOT_RESCUE_AT_ENTRY |\\n| growth_40pct | FUNDED_CONDITIONAL | None | 6.76% | NO_RESCUE_NEEDED |\\n| phase2_cash_5cr | FUNDED_CONDITIONAL | None | -1.92% | NO_RESCUE_NEEDED |\\n| phase2_cash_10cr | CASH_BREACH | 2027-03-19 | Not calculated | UNCOMMITTED_MINIMUM_WHOLE_SHARE_RESCUE |\\n| associate_zero | FUNDED_CONDITIONAL | None | -2.00% | NO_RESCUE_NEEDED |\\n| borrowing_rate_18pct | FUNDED_CONDITIONAL | None | -1.81% | NO_RESCUE_NEEDED |\\n| wacc_18pct | FUNDED_CONDITIONAL | None | -5.35% | NO_RESCUE_NEEDED |\\n| terminal_g_2pct | FUNDED_CONDITIONAL | None | -3.10% | NO_RESCUE_NEEDED |\\n| terminal_roic_15pct | FUNDED_CONDITIONAL | None | -3.84% | NO_RESCUE_NEEDED |\\n| terminal_collection_timing_30pct | FUNDED_CONDITIONAL | None | -2.50% | NO_RESCUE_NEEDED |\\n| no_sales_growth | FUNDED_CONDITIONAL | None | -20.13% | NO_RESCUE_NEEDED |\\n\\n\\nThere are **475 unique fully retained paths** in `model_output.json`, with event-level balances in `cash_event_ledger.csv`. Base/bull are not probability estimates; no expected-return average is calculated. Better terminal value does not override a prior cash shortage.\\n\\n## 11. Bear case and permanent-loss path\\n\\nThe serious bear case is not merely a lower exit multiple. Distributor receivables and slow inventory can expand while marketplace promotions, sales returns and quick-commerce losses consume cash. Restricted IPO funds cannot necessarily fund an unrelated acquisition or debt recall. Supplier terms can shorten; demand facilities can be recalled; a PhaseII commitment can absorb remaining free cash. An equity raise may be unauthorized, unavailable or heavily dilutive precisely when it is needed.\\n\\nAt the illustrative₹420 entry, a100%,90%,75% or50% equity loss corresponds to₹0,₹42,₹105 or₹210 per share. These are distinct distress-recovery illustrations, not modeled going-concern targets or assigned probabilities. A cash-breaching scenario receives **no ordinary terminal IRR**. Actual restructuring recoveries depend on creditors, inventory recoverability, IP ownership, transaction terms and new financing; those inputs are ND.\\n\\nA valuation loss can also occur without insolvency: revenue grows, but margin/CAC deteriorates, reinvestment returns converge toward WACC and the market stops paying a premium. A portfolio cannot assume exit at the last printed price in an illiquid SME lot. Financial loss and liquidity lock-in are separate risks.\\n\\n## 12. Five- and ten-bagger feasibility\\n\\nA5x price outcome over five years requires about37.97% annualized total return;10x requires58.49%. Over ten years,5x requires17.46% and10x25.89%. These are arithmetic hurdles, not forecasts. With initial10,644,344 shares plus the300,000-option stress, share count rises about2.82% before any rescue; an unchanged per-share target therefore requires more aggregate equity value.\\n\\nAt₹420,5x/10x prices are₹2,100/₹4,200. With10,944,344 stressed shares, required terminal equity values are approximately₹2,298.31/₹4,596.62 crore. That is not achieved merely by saying the beauty market is large. It requires a far larger cash-generative core business or an exceptional sustained valuation, after all working capital, capex, acquisition spending and dilution. At an illustrative25x terminal earnings multiple, those equity values require about₹91.93/₹183.86 crore earnings; that multiple is a feasibility illustration, not the valuation method used above.\\n\\nKey necessary conditions are credible retained cohorts, positive contribution after returns/marketing, distributor cash conversion, perfected brand rights, profitable new channels and incremental returns above financing cost. No verified evidence currently supports assigning a high probability to a5x or10x outcome. Ten-year forecast precision would exceed the available operating history.\\n\\n## 13. Monitoring plan\\n\\n| Item / trigger | Evidence required | Interpretation |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Corrected audit package | Final CARO, explanation of draft text, remuneration scope and prior edit-log remediation | Necessary for G2, not automatically sufficient. |\\n| Cash and tax bridge | Audited reconciliation of advances, taxes paid, restated comparators and quarterly bank returns | Do not substitute management reassurance for a numbered bridge. |\\n| IPO use | CARE/other monitoring report and object-wise bank balances through latest quarter | Distinguish spent, committed, restricted and genuinely free cash. |\\n| Funding | Facility limits, maturity/recall rights, security and covenant compliance; current term schedule | No optional renewal treated as committed. |\\n| Acquisition | Signed SPA, paid/unpaid consideration, milestones, funding source, acquired audited accounts and related parties | Reassess PhaseII cash and associate value; do not assume control/consolidation. |\\n| Channel cohorts | Website cohorts at3/6/12/24 months, CAC and contribution after returns; FOFO openings/closures/payback with full denominator | Test brand economics, not just gross sales. |\\n| Working capital | AR aging and collections; distributor stock/returns; inventory aging/write-offs; MSME payment history | Re-run the monthly cash path if end ARdays>80 or inventorydays>160, analytical alerts not covenants. |\\n| IP and RPTs | Registered assignments, consideration/royalties, arm's-length rental and remuneration reconciliations | Determine ownership and minority economics. |\\n| Dilution | ESOP grants, exercise terms and authorized-capital changes | Update cash proceeds and fully diluted denominator only with evidence. |\\n| Tradability | Full official20/60-session dataset, spreads/circuits and exchange restrictions | Keep L/IAS NS until tradeability is adequately known. |\\n\\nNo automatic monitoring or external outreach is established by this report. These are research checkpoints for the next filing cycle and any material event.\\n\\n## 14. Final classification and conditional action\\n\\n**Classification: Hold, unresolved / insufficient evidence. Not a verified compounder candidate, not a live buy/sell recommendation and not an investability rank.** Positive evidence is substantial reported core-product growth, widening channel access and stronger recent reported profitability. Negative evidence is weak cumulative cash conversion, unresolved statement/control contradictions, concentration, incomplete cohort economics and unknown current funding deployment.\\n\\nAt₹420, a ₹5 lakh reference position cannot be implemented exactly in800-share lots. One lot costs₹3.36 lakh; two lots₹6.72 lakh exceed the reference. The19 October secondary row reports17 trades,₹59.81 lakh turnover and13,600 deliverable shares. One lot is5.88% of that reported deliverable quantity, which is **not proven total volume or market depth**. No20/60-day exit-days metric is calculated from18 observed rows, and absent rows are never zero-trade days. Current price bands/restrictions are not inferred from security-master codes without their dictionary.\\n\\nThe useful next action is to obtain the specified primary reconciliations and current funding/liquidity data, then update the same model and gates. A lower share price alone does not cure a reliability or cash-data gap. Conversely, a documented correction should be evaluated on evidence rather than treated as a permanent stigma.\\n\\n## 15. Sources, assumptions and open questions\\n\\nEvidence labels: **V** verified historical disclosure; **C** reproducible calculation; **M** management statement; **A** analyst assumption. ND means not disclosed/located, NM not meaningful, NA genuinely inapplicable, NS not scoreable. Future model parameters are A even when inspired by a management ambition. Source availability as of20 October is distinguished from retrieval on23 October; later outcomes are excluded.\\n\\nPrimary material and exact locators are registered in `source_register.json`, with original-byte hashes for documents actually downloaded. The archive deliberately contains authored observations and public citations, not copyrighted original PDFs, OCR/text dumps, transport credentials or private file identifiers.\\n\\n- [S01: FY2025–26 annual report](https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0477/3327/6826/files/Recode_05th_AGM_Notice_Annual_Report_for_the_FY_2025-26.pdf?v=1788870583),8 September2026; financials audited30 May2026; audit PDF120–133, statements134–136, notes137–157.\\n- [S02: Final prospectus](https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0477/3327/6826/files/FINAL_PROSPECTUS_RSL.pdf?v=1778249586),May2026; issue/objects, business/concentration/IP and full restated financial/notes sections. Page locators in the report refer to PDF pages unless stated; printed pages are generally three lower.\\n- [S03: FY2024–25 annual report](https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0477/3327/6826/files/Annual_Report_2024-25.pdf?v=1778332302), original statements and audit PDF39–47.\\n- [S04: FY2021–22 financial statements](https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0477/3327/6826/files/21-22_-_FINANCIAL_STATEMENT.pdf?v=1766659762), stub period and actual original audit.\\n- [S05: FY2022–23 posted director report pack](https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0477/3327/6826/files/22-23_-_DIRECTOR_REPORT.pdf?v=1766659941), incomplete original audit coverage.\\n- [S06: FY2023–24 original financial statements](https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0477/3327/6826/files/23-24_FINANCIAL_STATEMENT.pdf?v=1766660031), including actual audit-trail exception.\\n- [S07: 3 June2026 earnings call transcript](https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0477/3327/6826/files/Recode_trans_merged.pdf?v=1780902387), complete management/Q&A review; statements remain M.\\n- [S08: 19 October interoperability security master](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/interop/NSE_CM_security_19102026.csv.gz), exact issuer/share/lot match, not a quote.\\n- [S09: IIFL historical data](https://www.indiainfoline.com/company/recode-studios-ltd-historical-data),18 observed pre-cutoff rows and conditional price; secondary.\\n- [S10: Issuer corporate-announcement index](https://shop.recodestudios.com/pages/corporate-announcement), discovery of monitoring/ESOP/acquisition filings; inaccessible underlying BSE documents not treated as read.\\n- [S11: HUL FY26 Beauty & Wellbeing disclosure](https://hul-performance-highlights.hul.co.in/performance-highlights-fy-2025-2026/segments/beauty/), peer economics context only.\\n- [S12: Honasa official annual-report index](https://honasa.in/pages/annual-reports), comparator source index.\\n- [S13: FSN/Nykaa investor relations](https://www.nykaa.com/investor-relations/lp), comparator source index.\\n\\nRemaining high-impact unknowns are current unrestricted cash, actual IPO deployment and deviation reporting, acquisition payment/phase-II obligations, current encumbrances, complete liquidity, option grants, ownership-perfected trademarks, channel contribution/retention cohorts, tax and advance cash bridges, and a complete FY23 original audit. None are set to zero or average scores.\\n\\nReconstruction: `python3 build_research.py` then `python3 verify_research.py` from the extracted folder. Both compact and full editions contain every authored input and all computation code. Generated outputs and validation can be deleted and regenerated without internet or source-original access. This establishes arithmetic and recovery reproducibility, **not truth of an unresolved disclosure, actual liquidity, or future returns**.\\n\"","\"number\"","\"content_markdown\"","1","\"Executive verdict\"","\"\\n**Hold: unresolved financial reliability, current funding and tradability. BQS NS; GQS NS, subject to a 5/10 cap; IAS NS.** Recode has genuine reported cosmetics sales growth, but a strong revenue chart is not yet proof of a durable, cash-generative brand franchise. FY26 revenue was ₹79.95 crore, reported PAT ₹11.22 crore and CFO ₹4.34 crore. The four-year FY23–FY26 reported CFO/PAT ratio is only 3.71%, with mixed restatement vintages explicitly retained. A more favorable three-year FY24–FY26 window gives 39.46%, illustrating why the start date must not be selected opportunistically. [S01, PDF134–136; S02, PDF190–194]\\n\\nThe decisive issues are unusually concrete: FY26 CARO retains an unresolved draft instruction; FY24's original audit flags three branches without an edit-log feature; FY26 bank submissions differ materially from books; cash-flow advances and tax payments lack a clean bridge; and the IPO deployment/current acquisition funding position is not independently established. These are reliability and diligence concerns, **not an allegation of fraud**. The main FY26 financial-statement and IFC opinions are unmodified. Both statements must be kept in view. [S01, PDF120–133,152; S06, PDF1–5]\\n\\nAt the secondary ₹420 reference, current-share market capitalization is ₹447.06 crore and historical P/E approximately 39.83x. This is not a bargain established by the IPO-era EPS denominator. All projected outcomes below are conditional, and no ordinary return is reported for a cash-breaching path. There is no live investability score or probability-weighted target.\\n\\n\"","2","\"Research header, gates and data confidence\"","\"\\n| Item | Treatment |\\n|---|---|\\n| Exact issuer | Recode Studios Limited, CIN L74999PB2021PLC053619; ISIN INE2B6701015; BSE SME RECODE, scrip 544755. Not a software company or another “Recode.” |\\n| History | Incorporated 16 June 2021; converted to public company 16 September 2025; listing date 12 May 2026. [S01/S02] |\\n| Capital | 10,644,344 current issued shares; 800-share lot confirmed by official 19 October interoperability security master. The master is not an NSE listing or an exchange quote. [S08] |\\n| Price | ₹420 on 19 October from IIFL historical HTML, secondary and conditional. Official BSE bhavcopy unavailable with ordinary HTTP403. [S09/X01] |\\n| Scope | FY22 stub and FY23–FY26 standalone accounts; FY23–FY25 restated prospectus figures are separate from annual-report comparators. A post-year 33% associate is not consolidated at 100%. |\\n| Horizon / hurdle | Five years, 19 October 2026–19 October 2031; default 15% nominal annualized before investor tax. WACC is separately assumed, not equated with that hurdle. |\\n| Liquidity reference | ₹5 lakh, purely illustrative, not a suggested position. |\\n| Overall confidence | Low for investment conclusion. Medium for historical revenue trajectory; low for current cash, normalized earnings, incremental returns and liquidity. |\\n\\n| Gate | Status | What closes the gap |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| G1 Integrity / minority treatment | Conditional | Explain audit/control contradictions, related-party/IP assignments and remuneration; no substantiated ≤3 integrity override applied. |\\n| G2 Financial reliability | Hold | Corrected final CARO and reconciled bank, tax and cash-flow notes, with auditor-supported bridges. |\\n| G3 Solvency / funding | Hold | Current unrestricted and object-restricted bank cash; monitoring report; acquisition payment/phase-II commitments; facility maturity and covenant schedules. |\\n| G4 Tradability | Hold | Authoritative complete 20/60-session trading history, actual volume, zero-trade days, circuits/spread and current restrictions. |\\n\\nA modeled cash failure is not evidence that the company actually defaulted. Conversely, an assumed funded scenario cannot establish current solvency. The distinction is maintained throughout.\\n\\n\"","\"Scorecard and calculation audit\"","\"\\n| Factor | Score | Confidence | Evidence and rationale |\\n|---|---:|---|---|\\n| GOV | 4 | Medium | S01/S02/S06: Paid Section42 private-placement compliance penalties, FY24 edit-log exception, FY26 unfinished CARO drafting and bank mismatches constrain confidence. No proven fraud/minority abuse inferred. |\\n| MOAT | 4 | Medium | S02/S07: Brand and channel access, no independently proven retention/CAC advantage or proprietary manufacturing; trademark assignments not fully reflected in registry. |\\n| REV | 8 | Medium | S02/S01: Three-year FY23-FY26 comparable operating-revenue CAGR exceeds30%; anchor9 capped8 for fewer than5 relevant full years and Medium confidence. No extra trajectory point. |\\n| DUR | 6 | Medium | S02/S07: Beauty consumption and omnichannel reach support runway; marketplace competition, supplier concentration and unproved repeat economics constrain durability. |\\n| CAP | NS | Low | S01/S02/S07: Mechanical returns can be computed but cohort and incremental capital economics, current deployment and accounting bridges insufficient. |\\n| CASH | 3 | Medium | S01/S02: FY23-FY25 cumulative CFO negative; FY23-FY26 reported CFO only a small fraction of PAT. Latest cash bridge is unreconciled. Persistent weakness triggers GQS cap5. |\\n| BAL | NS | Low | S01/S02/S10: Audited March cash not current October cash; IPO deployment, acquisition payment terms and demand facilities not reconciled. |\\n| EXEC | 5 | Medium | S01/S02/S07: Sales expansion achieved; franchise versus dark-store pivot, warehouse future delivery and M&A require testing. |\\n| EARN | NS | Low | S01/S02: Restatements, cash-flow presentation and tax-note mismatch prevent high-confidence normalized earnings assessment. |\\n| R | NS | Low | S09/model: Conditional model returns only; price and cash path not sufficiently verified for live scoring. |\\n| D | NS | Low | S01/model: Permanent-loss paths identifiable, but current funding gap and acquisition commitments ND. |\\n| P | NS | Low | S01/S07/model: Price-implied expectations cannot be underwritten reliably: secondary price, unverified current funding and demanding reverse-growth/margin paths. Sensitivity robustness insufficient for live scoring. |\\n| L | NS | Low | S08/S09: Only18 secondary observed trading rows. Authoritative20/60-session liquidity and restrictions unavailable. |\\n\\nThe nine business factors and four investment factors above are the complete 13-factor register. The three composites remain distinct. Every multiplication uses Decimal inputs and ROUND_HALF_UP only at final display, with no renormalization of missing factors.\\n\\n- **BQS:** 0.20×4 + 0.20×4 + 0.12×8 + 0.10×6 + 0.12×NS + 0.10×3 + 0.08×NS + 0.08×5 = known contribution **3.86**, coverage **80%**, missing weight **20%**. Mathematical bounds **4.06–5.86**; result **NS**, not 4.8/10 or a rescaled average.\\n- **GQS:** 0.40×8 + 0.20×6 + 0.15×NS + 0.15×NS + 0.10×3 = known contribution **4.70**, coverage **70%**, missing weight **30%**. Uncapped bounds **5.00–7.70**. Persistent weak cash conversion triggers a **5** cap, collapsing admissible numeric bounds to **5.00–5.00**, but the missing inputs still make the result **NS**, not a 5 score.\\n- **IAS:** 0.25×NS BQS + 0.35×NS R + 0.20×NS D + 0.10×NS P + 0.10×NS L. Direct coverage **0%**, direct formal bounds **1–10**; nested BQS information narrows possible arithmetic to **1.765–8.965**, not a score. No live ranking.\\n\\nREV's preselected relevant window is FY23–FY26, four full-year observations and three years of growth. Revenue CAGR 52.87% and core-product CAGR 57.83% exceed the >30% anchor, but the score is capped at 8 for fewer than five comparable full years/Medium confidence. The mixed FY22 stub is not used to manufacture a five-year CAGR. No additional momentum point is added. No governance≤3 or proven G3-failure override is asserted; such caps are policy rules, not findings supported by these data. Exact terms, bounds and cap order are in `score_audit.json`. A GOV±1 judgment sensitivity leaves all missing factors NS: GOV3 invokes BQS/IAS cap3; GOV5 changes BQS bounds to4.26–6.06 without making it scoreable. A separate hypothetical proven G3 failure caps IAS3, again without imputing missing inputs. These are boundary tests, not new factual classifications.\\n\\n\"","\"Business overview\"","\"\\nRecode is an **own-brand cosmetics sourcing, trading and distribution business**. It is neither an outsourced manufacturer for other brands nor a proprietary technology platform. It sells face, eye, lip and body products and related items, with approximately 350 SKUs. Production is sourced from 11 domestic and six overseas manufacturers, including suppliers in Germany, Taiwan, China and Thailand; there is no demonstrated owned factory or manufacturing-process moat. Product quality, formulation consistency, inventory availability and supplier economics matter more than nominal asset-lightness. [S02, business section, PDF139–153]\\n\\nChannels are owned website/marketplaces, B2B distributors and retailers, franchise stores (FOFO) and company-operated stores (COCO). The consistent reported footprint is three COCO and 19 FOFO; another prospectus passage says 21 FOFO, so the count discrepancy is retained rather than harmonized without evidence. FOFO stores may also distribute to smaller shops. Consequently wholesale sell-in, franchise consumer retail sales and marketplace GMV are not interchangeable revenue measures. [S02, business/risk sections; S07, 3 June call]\\n\\n| Net-product revenue | FY23 | FY24 | FY25 | 9M Dec25 |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| COCO | 0.00 | 0.31 | 0.87 | 0.51 |\\n| FOFO sell-in | 0.80 | 3.32 | 10.96 | 13.27 |\\n| Online | 8.68 | 12.23 | 20.02 | 24.84 |\\n| B2B | 10.18 | 17.78 | 14.86 | 16.89 |\\n| Net products | 19.66 | 33.64 | 46.70 | 55.52 |\\n\\nThese are disclosed historical amounts, not channel contribution margins. FY24 and 9M component totals differ from stated product totals by ₹0.01 lakh rounding; some printed FY24 channel percentages do not match the amounts. Calculations use amounts and disclose the discrepancy. Packing/service/marketing-support revenues are separate: FY26 net products ₹77.31 crore, franchise/services ₹0.18 crore and packing ₹2.47 crore. FY23 revenue included ₹1.34 crore of marketing support, making product-only growth a useful cross-check. [S02, channel tables; S01, revenue note]\\n\\n\"","\"Moat and industry position\"","\"\\nThe plausible advantage is a recognizable affordable cosmetics brand with professional/influencer reach and a distributor/franchise network. It remains a **hypothesis of moderate brand strength, not an established economic moat**. Consumers can switch; external manufacturing is replicable; marketplace demand can be rented through promotions; and franchise rollout is not proof of attractive same-store returns.\\n\\nThe IP record is not fully cleanly vested in the listed company. Prospectus trademarks include registrations held by Lush Looks and Bhuvan Mahajan, with executed transfer instruments claimed but registry changes pending. Some applications, including PREP+PRIME, were opposed/objected. Registry perfection, assignment terms, consideration, exclusivity and future royalty rights require verification. No valuable exclusive formulation patent or owned production technology is established. This is an IP/governance diligence issue, not an assertion that sales are unlawful. [S02, trademark/risk and statutory information, PDF145–149 and272–275]\\n\\nSupplier concentration increased: top-five share roughly 47.35% FY23, 57.02% FY24, 64.41% FY25 and 69.93% in 9M Dec25; top-ten suppliers reached 87.43% in that stub. Top-ten customers represented about 39.65% of FY25 and 39.38% of 9M revenue. Sourcing leverage, currency exposure, formulation continuity and a disruption at a major supplier can outweigh nominal vendor count. No durable minimum-volume customer commitments were verified. [S02, concentration risk tables]\\n\\nThree relevant comparators are useful economically, not as a license to transplant multiples:\\n\\n1. **Honasa Consumer:** a listed digital-first/omnichannel house of beauty brands. Relevant for cohort repeat, channel inventory and brand reinvestment; broader brand portfolio and reporting history make direct SME valuation comparisons weak. [S12]\\n2. **FSN E-Commerce Ventures / Nykaa:** a beauty retail/platform/distribution operator with owned brands. Relevant both as a channel and competitor, but gross retail revenue, marketplace economics and own-brand economics must not be conflated. [S13]\\n3. **Hindustan Unilever's Beauty & Wellbeing/Lakmé ecosystem:** a superior scale/distribution benchmark, not a small-cap peer. FY26 segment disclosure reports 6% underlying sales growth, ₹14,990 crore revenue and 28% segment margin. Segment margin is not comparable to Recode's standalone EBITDA or PAT. Mature scale and route-to-market advantages explain why its economics cannot be assumed for a young brand. [S11]\\n\\nNo synchronized peer valuation dataset was verified. Peer P/E medians, market-share gains and a precise addressable-market share are therefore ND rather than invented.\\n\\n\"","\"Revenue growth and scale\"","\"\\n| Period | Revenue | PAT | CFO | Gross cash capex | Ending debt |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| FY22_stub | 11.11 | 0.44 | ND | ND | 1.38 |\\n| FY23 | 22.38 | 0.69 | -5.27 | 0.81 | 3.79 |\\n| FY24 | 36.82 | 0.27 | -1.64 | 1.84 | 7.85 |\\n| FY25 | 47.80 | 3.30 | 3.14 | 1.06 | 7.56 |\\n| 9M_Dec25 | 57.39 | 9.06 | 5.97 | 1.70 | 3.43 |\\n| FY26 | 79.95 | 11.22 | 4.34 | 1.83 | 6.19 |\\n\\nFY22 is the 9 September 2021–31 March 2022 operating stub, with cosmetics, electronics, healthcare/other trading mixed together. It is not a full-year pure-beauty baseline. FY23–FY25 are restated prospectus data. FY26 is current annual-report data; the 9M row is not annualized or added to FY26. Detailed original-versus-restated figures are preserved in `historical_inputs.json`. [S04; S02; S01]\\n\\nFY26 operating revenue rose 67.27% from FY25. Core product sales rose faster than total reported revenue across FY23–FY26, reducing the concern that growth is only a service/reclassification artifact. The expansion predates the post-FY26 Aflairza acquisition; the historical FY26 increase is therefore not consolidation-led. However, “organic” does not mean volume growth: audited unit volumes, price/mix, discounts, returns, same-store sales and new-store contributions are not separately reconciled. The defensible statement is **reported standalone, predominantly core-product expansion**, not a precise organic volume CAGR. [S01/S02]\\n\\nThe mix also changed sharply: FY26 gross face-product sales ₹52.97 crore versus ₹24.43 crore in FY25, while gross body-product sales fell from ₹10.69 crore to ₹6.88 crore. Net product sales deduct discounts, so gross category tables must not be mixed with net channel totals. Face strength could reflect hero products, distribution or promotions; sustainable share gain is not independently proven. [S01, Note20]\\n\\nManagement's June call indicated a roughly 50% FY27 revenue-growth ambition, later extending that ambition across three years in Q&A. It is an M-type statement, not an audited forecast or model entitlement. NewU modern-trade entry, Nykaa Now/Superstore and partner dark stores expand access but offer no verified cohort profitability yet. Historical growth gets credit; unearned future margins do not. [S07; S01 front business review; S10 announcement index]\\n\\n\"","7","\"Growth quality and reinvestment runway\"","\"\\nAsset-light manufacturing does not mean capital-light growth. Recode must finance receivables, inventory, marketing, supplier commitments and physical distribution; capital can migrate from factory PPE into working capital and acquisitions. FY26 receivables rose from ₹8.09 crore to ₹15.38 crore, about 90%, above revenue growth. Ending receivable days rose from roughly 61.8 to70.2, while average-balance days are a different metric and must not be used to conceal year-end expansion. Inventory averaged roughly118 days of FY26 COGS. [S01/S02; reproducible calculations]\\n\\nThe mechanical NOPAT/ending-capital and incremental ΔNOPAT/Δcapital ratios are in `financial_calculations.json`. Operating invested capital is independently reconstructed from AR, inventory, operating advances, operating accruals/employee provisions and PPE/capital advances, excluding taxes, IPOprepayments and financing interest. Financing-minus-operating residuals, average ROIC/ROCE and incremental returns are retained, not forced to zero. Both before-interest FCF and CFO less actual financing-classified interest and capex are shown. They are not accepted as reliable cohort returns: profits jumped from a small FY24 base, tax/restatement vintages differ, IPO capital arrived after the anchor, supplier/customer financing changed, and current cash deployment is unknown. High apparent incremental returns driven by margin recovery do not prove that the next ₹1 reinvested will earn the same return. CAP remains NS.\\n\\n| Year | Operating IC | Financing IC residual | Average ROIC | Incremental ROIC | After-interest/capex cash |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| FY23 | 8.31 | 0.23 | NS | NS | -6.43 |\\n| FY24 | 12.91 | 0.25 | 8.03% | -1.41% | -4.34 |\\n| FY25 | 15.76 | -0.25 | 28.72% | 114.38% | 0.88 |\\n| FY26 | 24.52 | 0.35 | 58.09% | 86.59% | 1.84 |\\n\\nAll capital returns use an analytical25% tax rate. FY23 lacks a comparable opening pure-brand operating-capital base, so average/incremental entries are NS rather than fabricated. Residuals reflect tax balances, IPOprepay, financing interest and accounting classifications; they are not operating profits.\\n\\nThe June call supplies useful but incomplete operating leads:\\n- A claimed 40% monthly repeat rate applies to the website, not all channels or a multi-year customer cohort.\\n- Average order value was described as roughly ₹800 versus ₹700. Product mix, discounting and retention contribution are not independently bridged.\\n- Management gave inconsistent website shares of online sales, above70% in one passage and roughly50% in another. Do not select the higher figure.\\n- Customer acquisition cost was not properly attributable because influencer links lacked consistent tracking. A marketing-spend/revenue proxy is not CAC or lifetime-value evidence.\\n- Franchise monthly retail sales of ₹4–5 lakh and a22-month payback were management claims, not store-level audited cash cohorts. Franchise fees, working inventory, closures and partner returns require full denominator data.\\n- The company pivoted away from new FOFO expansion toward partner/company dark stores after the prospectus emphasized franchise rollout. The changed capital/opex burden needs a revised plan.\\n- Quick commerce was described as currently loss-making. A sale-or-return arrangement discussed for Nykaa transfers economic risk differently from a final retail sale. Promotional giveaways may dispose of slow stock but do not demonstrate zero economic obsolescence. [S07, complete call]\\n\\nIPO proceeds are treated as object-earmarked cash in the model, not asserted to be legally frozen bank balances; actual object utilization and restrictions remain unverified. IPO net proceeds were ₹33.6073 crore: warehouse ₹5.7420, brand/marketing ₹5.4090, working capital ₹19.5000 and GCP ₹2.9563. Marketing is an operating cost, not an additional capex deduction after it has already reduced modeled margin. The warehouse's total project cost ₹6.2810 crore includes ₹0.2500 crore already advanced at March2026; remaining cash outlay is ₹6.0310 crore, with remaining internal funding₹0.2890 crore. The model avoids charging the prior advance twice. [S02, objects PDF94 onward; S01 capex notes]\\n\\nAflairza's PhaseI33% investment at ₹8.32 crore is mentioned in the annual report; PhaseII51% is conditional on milestones. Target predecessor revenue and future consolidation claims are not accepted without acquired financial statements and transaction terms. A33% stake does not authorize100% revenue consolidation. The model gives no acquired operating earnings, stress-tests additional cash only and permits a separately haircut terminal recovery value. [S01 business review; X05 inaccessible transaction filing]\\n\\n\"","\"Management, governance and execution\"","\"\\nPromoters named on the offer document are Dheeraj Bansal, Rahul Sachdeva, Shelly Bansal, Shalini Trehan, Preeti Trehan and Karan Bansal; responsibilities, remuneration and related parties are described in the prospectus. Post-IPO promoter ownership was about65.01% in the offer document. A later September ownership figure and current pledges/encumbrances were not independently verified, so no zero-pledge assertion is made. [S02, capital and management sections]\\n\\nThe15 December2025 bonus of780 for1 changed10,424 shares into8,141,144 and is not economic dilution. The IPO fresh issue added2,503,200 shares;319,200 OFS shares transferred existing ownership and provided **no company cash**. At₹158, fresh gross proceeds are₹39.55056 crore and OFS₹5.04336 crore. A2023 allotment of424 shares at₹95,700 before the bonus equates to about₹122.54 per post-bonus share, not₹95,700 in the current denominator. [S02 capital history]\\n\\nFY26 director compensation was about₹1.6925 crore, approximately15.1% of reported PAT, before other related-party family salary/rent items. Related-party rental payments to four named family counterparties total about₹0.1638 crore. The FY25 employee-cost director figure₹1.0050 crore differs from the RPT total₹1.1610 crore; the FY26₹0.0001 crore difference looks like rounding but the prior-year difference is material. These require reconciliation and arm's-length assessment, not an automatic abuse allegation. [S01, Notes23/31]\\n\\nA300,000-option ESOP scheme is reported, but current grants, vesting, exercise prices and fully diluted obligations were not verified in a primary accessible filing. The model conservatively stress-adds300,000 shares, assumes **no exercise cash**, and does not describe all scheme capacity as granted. Authorized11,000,000 shares minus issued10,644,344 and that reserve leaves55,656 shares of theoretical rescue capacity. It is not permission or a commitment to issue shares. [S01 capital note; X04 unavailable]\\n\\nExecution is mixed: strong historical sales and channel expansion are visible, but warehouse commissioning, dark-store economics, acquisitions and controls remain to be demonstrated. The prospectus discloses Section42 non-compliance in the2023 private placement: subscription money used before formal Board allotment, a third-party remittance mismatch and late PAS3. ROC orders of18 December2025 imposed company penalties totaling₹2.23 lakh and₹1.23 lakh each on Rahul Sachdeva and Dheeraj Bansal, disclosed as paid. This is a documented governance lapse, not merely speculative risk, but does not by itself establish fraud. The company was claimant in a₹4.03 lakh criminal case; two promoter tax demands totaled₹1.38 lakh. At31 December2025, disclosed bank guarantees/tax/other contingencies were nil, but a₹2.15 crore warehouse-land commitment existed; it is within the warehouse project, not an additional double-counted commitment. [S02, printed30–32,60 and262 onward]\\n\\nThe FY26 auditor change to RMSG from the earlier Samarth M. Surana firm is recorded; no adverse resignation motive is inferred without the actual transition documents.\\n\\n\"","9","\"Financial forensics\"","\"\\n### Assurance history and annexures\\n\\n| Year/source | What was actually reviewed | Interpretation |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| FY22 original S04 PDF4–7 | Samarth M. Surana/Bharat Rattan,21 September2022; unmodified BS/P&L/notes opinion; CARO exemption; no standalone positive IFC report | Does not audit a cash-flow statement that was not provided. |\\n| FY23 posted S05 pack |15-page Board/AGM/tax material, no actual statutory audit or complete financial statements | Board's assertion of no qualification is not independent original-audit review. Prospectus restatement coverage is separate. |\\n| FY24 original S06 PDF1–5 | Unmodified BS/P&L/notes opinion dated2 September2024; CARO exempt; three branches lacked edit logs, auditor unable to comment there; proper-books reporting excepts that issue | Historic audit-trail weakness survives any later unmodified restatement language. No separate IFC assurance inferred. |\\n| FY25 original S03 PDF39–47 | Unmodified BS/P&L/CFO opinion; CARO read; no default/fraud/fresh allotment/loans or investments/resignation during that year reported; edit logs operated and were preserved | Statements are audit conclusions within their scope, not guarantees. Generic internal-control responsibilities are not a positive IFC opinion. |\\n| Prospectus S02 PDF186–194 and notes | RMSG restated FY23–FY25/9MDec25 report and statements, with restatement reconciliation | Restated figures deliberately differ from originals; this does not erase old control exceptions. |\\n| FY26 S01 PDF120–133 | Unmodified main and IFC opinions; CARO and legal reporting read in full | Unfinished CARO drafting, remuneration scope limitation and inconsistent clauses require correction before clean-reliability conclusion. |\\n\\n### Material contradictions and what not to do\\n\\n1. **CARO draft residue:** FY26 retains a draft-review instruction relating to agreeing highlighted conclusions with audit evidence. Separately, the auditor could not comment on adequacy of profits under Section197(16) without conversion-date statements. Do not compress this into “clean audit.” [S01, PDF122/124]\\n2. **Bank statements versus books:** March2026 reported net-current-asset book value₹21.3850 crore versus bank submission₹19.0966 crore, gap₹2.2884 crore. June2025 gap₹3.3755 crore; other quarter differences change direction and size. CARO's language on working-capital limits below₹5 crore does not reconcile the actual disclosure. [S01, Note35 PDF152]\\n3. **Tax mismatch:** tax note total₹3.6942 crore includes a prior-period credit₹0.0916 crore, while the P&L tax₹3.7858 crore and PAT₹11.2248 crore do not. Do not silently boost PAT by the credit. [S01, PDF135/148]\\n4. **Cash-flow anomalies:** advances are a₹1.6915 crore inflow even though short-term advances increase by₹1.8585 crore. Cash tax₹7.6982 crore is far above current tax charge₹3.8962 crore and not explained by tax-provision changes. Offsetting reclassifications are possible. Reported CFO is retained and confidence reduced; “corrected CFO” is ND. [S01, PDF134/136]\\n5. **Equity wording:** CARO refers to private placement despite a bonus-only FY26 share reconciliation. One note calls₹158 the premium, whereas issue terms clearly specify₹158 total and₹148 premium. Use the verified issue terms and keep the contradictory wording. [S01, PDF128/141; S02]\\n6. **Restatements:** FY23 PAT₹0.7009→₹0.6928 crore; FY24₹0.4060→₹0.2743; FY25₹3.1118→₹3.3029. Tax, MSME interest, prior items and small depreciation/FX changes explain disclosed adjustments. FY26 opening retained earnings carries₹0.0514 crore reconciliation, while comparators remain largely original. FY25 CFO₹3.1397 crore restated versus₹3.0245 crore annual comparator. [S02 restatement notes; S01]\\n7. **Supplier financing:** MSME interest payable₹0.1133 crore versus₹0.0487 crore, with expense₹0.0244 crore not alone bridging the change. Supplier credit is not free capital. [S01, Note34]\\n\\nFY26 CFO is before financing-classified interest. From₹4.3378 crore CFO, deduct₹1.8282 crore cash capex,₹0.6722 crore interest and₹1.3686 crore principal to get about₹0.4688 crore before the₹0.0265 crore net securities proceeds. This reconciles approximately₹0.4953 crore cash increase, subject to₹0.0001 crore rounding. CFO/PAT is therefore not after-interest owner cash. Gross cash capex comprises₹1.5782 crore PPE additions plus₹0.2500 crore capital advances; no acquisition is buried in that audited FY26 bridge. [S01, PDF136 and PPE notes]\\n\\nReceivables more than six months overdue were₹0.8188 crore, about5.3% of total, versus₹0.6886 crore/about8.5% previously. Rising absolute balances do not mean every aging percentage worsened. More than one year was₹0.3918 crore. Inventory obsolescence, returns and distributor stock require cohort evidence beyond a management statement about giveaways. [S01, receivable aging]\\n\\nMarch2026 debt₹6.1927 crore includes term/current maturities₹2.9369 crore and demand borrowing₹3.2558 crore; cash₹1.3170 crore gives net debt₹4.8757 crore. Cash consists of bank balances₹1.0601 crore and cash on hand₹0.2569 crore. IPO money later raised cannot simply be added to March cash and called October net cash. Facility renewals are not committed long-term finance. Operating leases are already expensed under AS; no lease-debt addback is made without adjusting rent. [S01, notes3–19 and loan maturity note]\\n\\n\"","10","\"Valuation and market expectations\"","\"\\nAt the secondary reference, market cap is₹447.06 crore. FY26 PAT divided by10,644,344 current shares gives EPS₹10.55, not the report's pre-IPO₹13.79. P/E is approximately39.83x. A March balance-sheet EV combined with an October price would be a mixed-date statistic; no falsely precise current EV or “ex-IPO cash” multiple is claimed.\\n\\n### Cash-linked model, not a free-standing terminal multiple\\n\\nThe model begins at the audited31 March2026 balance sheet and explicitly bridges pre-entry dates through19 October, then60 holding months to19 October2031. Sales, collections, AR, inventory, supplier balances, other working capital, tax, interest, maintenance/growth capex, warehouse spend, dividends, acquisitions, debt maturities and restricted cash releases are linked. Each event pays outlays after an assumed early collection fraction and before late collections. A minimum cash breach ends that path; later profits cannot retroactively repair it.\\n\\nAll interim operating assumptions are **A**, not actual FY27 results. Base entry revenue run-rate₹119.92 crore rises to₹135 crore after12 holding months and22% annually thereafter; bear₹95→₹100 crore/6%; bull₹125→₹160 crore/30%. These are run-rates, not claimed annual revenue forecasts. The model decomposes each annual exit run-rate into25% own-site,25% marketplace,18% FOFO sell-in,30% B2B and2% COCO, with own-site orders at₹800 AOV. This is an explicit feasibility exercise, not a verified channel mix or consumer cohort. No retail/wholesale double count is used.\\n\\n| Parameter | Bear | Base | Bull |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\\n| EBITDA margin |12%|20%|23%|\\n| COGS/revenue |45%|40%|38%|\\n| AR days |100|65|52|\\n| Inventory days |190|130|100|\\n| Payable days |30|50|60|\\n| Early share of collections |70%|93%|98%|\\n| Terminal WACC |18%|15%|13%|\\n| Terminal growth |2%|4%|5%|\\n| Terminal ROIC ceiling |15%|25%|30%|\\n| Associate cost recovery |0%|50%|100%|\\n\\nMaintenance capex is1.2% of sales; growth capex is3.5% of the excess annual run-rate over the reference base, multiplied by elapsed-year fraction. Depreciation is1.2% of sales; tax25%; debt interest12%. These assumptions are independently visible in inputs, not calibrated to produce a target price. Warehouse remaining₹6.031 crore is scheduled byApril2027. Object funds are released only for matching expenses/working-capital additions; unused object cash is excluded from terminal shareholder cash. Net IPO proceeds exclude all issue fees, but₹0.4028 crore already prepaid before the anchor is reimbursed once to avoid double deduction. OFS provides no funding. The planned marketing object is released against operating expenses already inside margin, not deducted twice.\\n\\nThe PhaseI₹8.32 crore acquisition is assigned a23 July2026 cash event as an explicit timing assumption pending the SPA/bank evidence. Base and bull assume no mandatory PhaseII funding; bear assumes₹5 crore. Separate₹5/10 crore stresses test the unknown future commitment. A pre-entry₹0.25-per-share dividend (₹0.2661 crore) is a company cash outflow; an investor entering19 October does not receive it. No subsequent dividend is assumed.\\n\\n| Scenario | Cash status | First breach | Terminal price | Five-year IRR | Rescue result |\\n|---|---|---|---:|---:|---|\\n| bear | CASH_BREACH | 2026-04-30 | Not admissible | Not calculated | PREENTRY_BREACH_CANNOT_RESCUE_AT_ENTRY |\\n| base | FUNDED_CONDITIONAL | None in assumed path | 383.53 | -1.80% | NO_RESCUE_NEEDED |\\n| bull | FUNDED_CONDITIONAL | None in assumed path | 872.77 | 15.75% | NO_RESCUE_NEEDED |\\n\\n\\nTerminal enterprise value is earned from **forward12-month NOPAT ×(1−g/earned incremental ROIC)/(WACC−g)**. Forward NOPAT is the exact sum of monthly ramped sales less operating costs, depreciation and assumed tax, not terminal run-rate NOPAT mechanically multiplied by(1+g). ROIC is the lower of the scenario ceiling and terminal NOPAT divided by actual modeled operating invested capital. Reinvestment is not set to zero while growth remains positive. Add only free cash above the₹0.25 crore operating floor and a haircut associate value; subtract remaining debt. A further12-month monthly cash continuation, with no new financing or object release, proves terminal operating funding. It maintains working-capital/PPE intensity and adds productive-capital spending where needed to respect the conservative incremental ROIC ceiling. The explicit net investment equals Δworking capital + growth PPE + that additional investment; Gordon reinvestment uses this amount, not an unsupported profit payout. Required reserve is the cash floor plus the worst cumulative early/late-collection draw in that continuation, and is excluded from excess cash. Any continuation breach suppresses ordinary returns; subsequent continuation rows, if retained to diagnose required reserve, are explicitly marked post-breach counterfactual diagnostics and never a resumed funded path. No residual restricted object cash is counted as freely distributable.300,000 potential ESOP shares are included as a dilution stress without assuming exercise proceeds. Implied P/E and EV/EBITDA are outputs, never starting assumptions.\\n\\n### Whole-share rescue and return thresholds\\n\\nRescue is an **uncommitted counterfactual**, not ordinary financing. It can occur only at entry, after the pre-entry path survives, at30% discount to the tested share price with5% issue cost. Maximum55,656 whole shares reflects existing authorized capacity after the ESOP reserve, approximately₹1.55 crore net at₹420. Every candidate is rerun through the full cash path. Because operating cash flows are independent of the number of rescue shares, the solver uses exact cash linearity and Decimal ceiling to obtain the smallest whole-share candidate within the bound, then reruns that candidate and its adjacent failed share count. The no-rescue, maximum-capacity, minimum-success and adjacent-failure paths are all retained. Pre-entry failures cannot be repaired by issuing shares after the failure.\\n\\n| Scenario | Hurdle | Retested threshold | Funding status |\\n|---|---:|---:|---|\\n| bear | 12.00% | ND | NO_FUNDED_TERMINAL |\\n| bear | 15.00% | ND | NO_FUNDED_TERMINAL |\\n| bear | 18.00% | ND | NO_FUNDED_TERMINAL |\\n| base | 12.00% | 217.62 | CONDITIONAL_NOT_ACTIONABLE |\\n| base | 15.00% | 190.68 | CONDITIONAL_NOT_ACTIONABLE |\\n| base | 18.00% | 167.64 | CONDITIONAL_NOT_ACTIONABLE |\\n| bull | 12.00% | 495.23 | CONDITIONAL_NOT_ACTIONABLE |\\n| bull | 15.00% | 433.92 | CONDITIONAL_NOT_ACTIONABLE |\\n| bull | 18.00% | 381.49 | CONDITIONAL_NOT_ACTIONABLE |\\n\\nThese are conditional arithmetic thresholds, not buy prices. The financing model is retested at each threshold because a lower subscription price can reduce rescue cash. The hurdle is an equity-return assumption, not the WACC. No precise entry threshold becomes actionable while G2/G3/G4 remain unresolved.\\n\\n| Hurdle | Joint price/dilution bracket for PhaseII10cr stress | Status |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| 12.00% | 214.5552 to 214.5554 | JOINT_PRICE_DILUTION_BRACKET |\\n| 15.00% | 187.8975 to 187.8976 | JOINT_PRICE_DILUTION_BRACKET |\\n| 18.00% | 165.1037 to 165.1038 | JOINT_PRICE_DILUTION_BRACKET |\\n\\nFor the₹10 crore PhaseII stress, entry price and whole-share rescue dilution are additionally solved jointly. Every price trial recalculates the minimum shares and reruns the cash path; a price below the financing floor is inadmissible even if a naïve terminal-price division suggests a high return. Integer dilution can produce tiny jumps, so bounded price intervals are reported instead of spurious exact roots.\\n\\n### Reverse expectations and sensitivities\\n\\n| Hurdle | Conditional growth bracket | Status |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| 12.00% | 50.65% to 50.65% | BOUNDED_CONDITIONAL_ROOT |\\n| 15.00% | 56.68% to 56.68% | BOUNDED_CONDITIONAL_ROOT |\\n| 18.00% | 62.67% to 62.67% | BOUNDED_CONDITIONAL_ROOT |\\n\\nReverse growth searches0–80% annual post-first-year growth. Sampled feasible/infeasible boundaries are refined before testing a return-root bracket, avoiding a false no-root conclusion from a coarse grid. All grid, boundary-refinement, bisection and failed funding trials remain in the losslessly deduplicated path register. A root is rejected if it crosses a cash infeasibility discontinuity; no algebraic growth root is accepted without a corresponding funded trajectory. Hurdle roots also retain integer rescue subtrials.\\n\\n| Stress | Ordinary cash result | First breach | Five-year IRR if funded | Bounded rescue |\\n|---|---|---|---:|---|\\n| margin_minus_3pp | FUNDED_CONDITIONAL | None | -5.60% | NO_RESCUE_NEEDED |\\n| margin_plus_3pp | FUNDED_CONDITIONAL | None | 1.49% | NO_RESCUE_NEEDED |\\n| AR_plus_30days | FUNDED_CONDITIONAL | None | -2.98% | NO_RESCUE_NEEDED |\\n| inventory_plus_60days | FUNDED_CONDITIONAL | None | -2.74% | NO_RESCUE_NEEDED |\\n| supplier_days_minus_20 | FUNDED_CONDITIONAL | None | -2.11% | NO_RESCUE_NEEDED |\\n| early_collections_70pct | CASH_BREACH | 2026-04-30 | Not calculated | PREENTRY_BREACH_CANNOT_RESCUE_AT_ENTRY |\\n| growth_40pct | FUNDED_CONDITIONAL | None | 6.76% | NO_RESCUE_NEEDED |\\n| phase2_cash_5cr | FUNDED_CONDITIONAL | None | -1.92% | NO_RESCUE_NEEDED |\\n| phase2_cash_10cr | CASH_BREACH | 2027-03-19 | Not calculated | UNCOMMITTED_MINIMUM_WHOLE_SHARE_RESCUE |\\n| associate_zero | FUNDED_CONDITIONAL | None | -2.00% | NO_RESCUE_NEEDED |\\n| borrowing_rate_18pct | FUNDED_CONDITIONAL | None | -1.81% | NO_RESCUE_NEEDED |\\n| wacc_18pct | FUNDED_CONDITIONAL | None | -5.35% | NO_RESCUE_NEEDED |\\n| terminal_g_2pct | FUNDED_CONDITIONAL | None | -3.10% | NO_RESCUE_NEEDED |\\n| terminal_roic_15pct | FUNDED_CONDITIONAL | None | -3.84% | NO_RESCUE_NEEDED |\\n| terminal_collection_timing_30pct | FUNDED_CONDITIONAL | None | -2.50% | NO_RESCUE_NEEDED |\\n| no_sales_growth | FUNDED_CONDITIONAL | None | -20.13% | NO_RESCUE_NEEDED |\\n\\n\\nThere are **475 unique fully retained paths** in `model_output.json`, with event-level balances in `cash_event_ledger.csv`. Base/bull are not probability estimates; no expected-return average is calculated. Better terminal value does not override a prior cash shortage.\\n\\n\"","11","\"Bear case and permanent-loss path\"","\"\\nThe serious bear case is not merely a lower exit multiple. Distributor receivables and slow inventory can expand while marketplace promotions, sales returns and quick-commerce losses consume cash. Restricted IPO funds cannot necessarily fund an unrelated acquisition or debt recall. Supplier terms can shorten; demand facilities can be recalled; a PhaseII commitment can absorb remaining free cash. An equity raise may be unauthorized, unavailable or heavily dilutive precisely when it is needed.\\n\\nAt the illustrative₹420 entry, a100%,90%,75% or50% equity loss corresponds to₹0,₹42,₹105 or₹210 per share. These are distinct distress-recovery illustrations, not modeled going-concern targets or assigned probabilities. A cash-breaching scenario receives **no ordinary terminal IRR**. Actual restructuring recoveries depend on creditors, inventory recoverability, IP ownership, transaction terms and new financing; those inputs are ND.\\n\\nA valuation loss can also occur without insolvency: revenue grows, but margin/CAC deteriorates, reinvestment returns converge toward WACC and the market stops paying a premium. A portfolio cannot assume exit at the last printed price in an illiquid SME lot. Financial loss and liquidity lock-in are separate risks.\\n\\n\"","12","\"Five- and ten-bagger feasibility\"","\"\\nA5x price outcome over five years requires about37.97% annualized total return;10x requires58.49%. Over ten years,5x requires17.46% and10x25.89%. These are arithmetic hurdles, not forecasts. With initial10,644,344 shares plus the300,000-option stress, share count rises about2.82% before any rescue; an unchanged per-share target therefore requires more aggregate equity value.\\n\\nAt₹420,5x/10x prices are₹2,100/₹4,200. With10,944,344 stressed shares, required terminal equity values are approximately₹2,298.31/₹4,596.62 crore. That is not achieved merely by saying the beauty market is large. It requires a far larger cash-generative core business or an exceptional sustained valuation, after all working capital, capex, acquisition spending and dilution. At an illustrative25x terminal earnings multiple, those equity values require about₹91.93/₹183.86 crore earnings; that multiple is a feasibility illustration, not the valuation method used above.\\n\\nKey necessary conditions are credible retained cohorts, positive contribution after returns/marketing, distributor cash conversion, perfected brand rights, profitable new channels and incremental returns above financing cost. No verified evidence currently supports assigning a high probability to a5x or10x outcome. Ten-year forecast precision would exceed the available operating history.\\n\\n\"","\"Monitoring plan\"","\"\\n| Item / trigger | Evidence required | Interpretation |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Corrected audit package | Final CARO, explanation of draft text, remuneration scope and prior edit-log remediation | Necessary for G2, not automatically sufficient. |\\n| Cash and tax bridge | Audited reconciliation of advances, taxes paid, restated comparators and quarterly bank returns | Do not substitute management reassurance for a numbered bridge. |\\n| IPO use | CARE/other monitoring report and object-wise bank balances through latest quarter | Distinguish spent, committed, restricted and genuinely free cash. |\\n| Funding | Facility limits, maturity/recall rights, security and covenant compliance; current term schedule | No optional renewal treated as committed. |\\n| Acquisition | Signed SPA, paid/unpaid consideration, milestones, funding source, acquired audited accounts and related parties | Reassess PhaseII cash and associate value; do not assume control/consolidation. |\\n| Channel cohorts | Website cohorts at3/6/12/24 months, CAC and contribution after returns; FOFO openings/closures/payback with full denominator | Test brand economics, not just gross sales. |\\n| Working capital | AR aging and collections; distributor stock/returns; inventory aging/write-offs; MSME payment history | Re-run the monthly cash path if end ARdays>80 or inventorydays>160, analytical alerts not covenants. |\\n| IP and RPTs | Registered assignments, consideration/royalties, arm's-length rental and remuneration reconciliations | Determine ownership and minority economics. |\\n| Dilution | ESOP grants, exercise terms and authorized-capital changes | Update cash proceeds and fully diluted denominator only with evidence. |\\n| Tradability | Full official20/60-session dataset, spreads/circuits and exchange restrictions | Keep L/IAS NS until tradeability is adequately known. |\\n\\nNo automatic monitoring or external outreach is established by this report. These are research checkpoints for the next filing cycle and any material event.\\n\\n\"","14","\"Final classification and conditional action\"","\"\\n**Classification: Hold, unresolved / insufficient evidence. Not a verified compounder candidate, not a live buy/sell recommendation and not an investability rank.** Positive evidence is substantial reported core-product growth, widening channel access and stronger recent reported profitability. Negative evidence is weak cumulative cash conversion, unresolved statement/control contradictions, concentration, incomplete cohort economics and unknown current funding deployment.\\n\\nAt₹420, a ₹5 lakh reference position cannot be implemented exactly in800-share lots. One lot costs₹3.36 lakh; two lots₹6.72 lakh exceed the reference. The19 October secondary row reports17 trades,₹59.81 lakh turnover and13,600 deliverable shares. One lot is5.88% of that reported deliverable quantity, which is **not proven total volume or market depth**. No20/60-day exit-days metric is calculated from18 observed rows, and absent rows are never zero-trade days. Current price bands/restrictions are not inferred from security-master codes without their dictionary.\\n\\nThe useful next action is to obtain the specified primary reconciliations and current funding/liquidity data, then update the same model and gates. A lower share price alone does not cure a reliability or cash-data gap. Conversely, a documented correction should be evaluated on evidence rather than treated as a permanent stigma.\\n\\n\"","\"Sources, assumptions and open questions\"","\"\\nEvidence labels: **V** verified historical disclosure; **C** reproducible calculation; **M** management statement; **A** analyst assumption. ND means not disclosed/located, NM not meaningful, NA genuinely inapplicable, NS not scoreable. Future model parameters are A even when inspired by a management ambition. Source availability as of20 October is distinguished from retrieval on23 October; later outcomes are excluded.\\n\\nPrimary material and exact locators are registered in `source_register.json`, with original-byte hashes for documents actually downloaded. The archive deliberately contains authored observations and public citations, not copyrighted original PDFs, OCR/text dumps, transport credentials or private file identifiers.\\n\\n- [S01: FY2025–26 annual report](https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0477/3327/6826/files/Recode_05th_AGM_Notice_Annual_Report_for_the_FY_2025-26.pdf?v=1788870583),8 September2026; financials audited30 May2026; audit PDF120–133, statements134–136, notes137–157.\\n- [S02: Final prospectus](https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0477/3327/6826/files/FINAL_PROSPECTUS_RSL.pdf?v=1778249586),May2026; issue/objects, business/concentration/IP and full restated financial/notes sections. Page locators in the report refer to PDF pages unless stated; printed pages are generally three lower.\\n- [S03: FY2024–25 annual report](https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0477/3327/6826/files/Annual_Report_2024-25.pdf?v=1778332302), original statements and audit PDF39–47.\\n- [S04: FY2021–22 financial statements](https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0477/3327/6826/files/21-22_-_FINANCIAL_STATEMENT.pdf?v=1766659762), stub period and actual original audit.\\n- [S05: FY2022–23 posted director report pack](https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0477/3327/6826/files/22-23_-_DIRECTOR_REPORT.pdf?v=1766659941), incomplete original audit coverage.\\n- [S06: FY2023–24 original financial statements](https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0477/3327/6826/files/23-24_FINANCIAL_STATEMENT.pdf?v=1766660031), including actual audit-trail exception.\\n- [S07: 3 June2026 earnings call transcript](https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0477/3327/6826/files/Recode_trans_merged.pdf?v=1780902387), complete management/Q&A review; statements remain M.\\n- [S08: 19 October interoperability security master](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/interop/NSE_CM_security_19102026.csv.gz), exact issuer/share/lot match, not a quote.\\n- [S09: IIFL historical data](https://www.indiainfoline.com/company/recode-studios-ltd-historical-data),18 observed pre-cutoff rows and conditional price; secondary.\\n- [S10: Issuer corporate-announcement index](https://shop.recodestudios.com/pages/corporate-announcement), discovery of monitoring/ESOP/acquisition filings; inaccessible underlying BSE documents not treated as read.\\n- [S11: HUL FY26 Beauty & Wellbeing disclosure](https://hul-performance-highlights.hul.co.in/performance-highlights-fy-2025-2026/segments/beauty/), peer economics context only.\\n- [S12: Honasa official annual-report index](https://honasa.in/pages/annual-reports), comparator source index.\\n- [S13: FSN/Nykaa investor relations](https://www.nykaa.com/investor-relations/lp), comparator source index.\\n\\nRemaining high-impact unknowns are current unrestricted cash, actual IPO deployment and deviation reporting, acquisition payment/phase-II obligations, current encumbrances, complete liquidity, option grants, ownership-perfected trademarks, channel contribution/retention cohorts, tax and advance cash bridges, and a complete FY23 original audit. None are set to zero or average scores.\\n\\nReconstruction: `python3 build_research.py` then `python3 verify_research.py` from the extracted folder. Both compact and full editions contain every authored input and all computation code. Generated outputs and validation can be deleted and regenerated without internet or source-original access. This establishes arithmetic and recovery reproducibility, **not truth of an unresolved disclosure, actual liquidity, or future returns**.\\n\"","\"heading_start\"","\"body_start\"","\"end\"","\"original_heading\"","408","432","2020","\"## 1. Executive verdict\\n\"","2069","4282","\"## 2. Research header, gates and data confidence\\n\"","4320","8575","\"## 3. Scorecard and calculation audit\\n\"","8599","10645","\"## 4. Business overview\\n\"","10678","13389","\"## 5. Moat and industry position\\n\"","13420","15708","\"## 6. Revenue growth and scale\\n\"","15753","20456","\"## 7. Growth quality and reinvestment runway\\n\"","20499","23617","\"## 8. Management, governance and execution\\n\"","23643","28988","\"## 9. Financial forensics\\n\"","29029","39065","\"## 10. Valuation and market expectations\\n\"","39106","40358","\"## 11. Bear case and permanent-loss path\\n\"","40398","41743","\"## 12. Five- and ten-bagger feasibility\\n\"","41766","43771","\"## 13. Monitoring plan\\n\"","43822","45202","\"## 14. Final classification and conditional action\\n\"","45249","49265","\"## 15. Sources, assumptions and open questions\\n\"","\"filename\"","\"format\"","\"content_sha256\"","\"model_inputs.json\"","\"e3dbf2dde516dc3922979afad943488dc5a7ba8999e9c5c21cc0de0f63cdfe8b\"","\"Complete JSON content\"","\"fd16ea30e1eea61fbf95eb7e1e7b3e2e6ac1bbd35d1603975622a1e5d90fad2f\"","\"Authored analytical table or source register, not a bundled original filing. Source-access qualifications remain in the research.\"","\"content\"","\"historical_inputs.json\"","\"a99966e8b2d3772a465bf9fd6a67e21731857792d9619ec65dd53da91dfc1d75\"","\"d84af13f50e787c10c2fd3483174996d660edb6db6acab18327cc2cba54c1458\"","\"factor_evidence.json\"","\"e54e5b5b1ec7d90a78cb45d39789c448b63dc47e3d8d793e5a398fe4c7aa8f94\"","\"bcda587fe062d45bd65cc6dec4e7dce5e23bc30619b309092127bf1ec07e63df\"","\"39a45b46023cdcd3618a7990ac080d3d18726859b8b88c6fe59081dfc29969fa\"","\"8a05ebdf86f7818e2d00400ae1fa679635a0d5c407